Probability of recapturing adult lizards
To assess capture saturation and sampling completeness across field seasons, we filter the dataset for adult lizards (mean snout-vent length \(&gt; 75\text{ mm}\)) and compute the cumulative number of unique individuals captured as a function of the day of the year (DOY) across sites and years.


Plot cummulative captures
# filter adults
adults_by_site_year &lt;- mr_data |&gt;
  group_by(site, year, sex, id) |&gt;
  summarise(avg_svl = mean(svl, na.rm = TRUE)) |&gt;
  filter(avg_svl &gt; 75) |&gt;
  ungroup()

# filter mark-recapture data to include only adults
mr_data_adults &lt;- mr_data |&gt;
  semi_join(adults_by_site_year, by = c("site", "year", "sex", "id"))

# plot
mr_data_adults |&gt;
  filter(
    year &gt; 2022,
    paste(site, year) != "HE 2025"
  ) |&gt;
  group_by(site, year, sex, id) |&gt;
  mutate(capture_number = row_number()) |&gt;
  ungroup() |&gt;
  group_by(site, year, doy) |&gt;
  summarise(
    n_first_capture = sum(capture_number == 1), .groups = "drop"
  ) |&gt;
  mutate(
    cummulative_captures = cumsum(n_first_capture)
  ) |&gt;
  ggplot(aes(x = doy, y = cummulative_captures, color = site)) +
  geom_line(linewidth = 1) +
  facet_wrap(~year, scales = "free_x") +
  labs(
    x = "Date",
    y = "Number of unique adult lizards"
  ) +
  scale_color_manual(values = site_colors) +
  scale_x_continuous(
    breaks = c(1, 32, 60, 91, 121, 152, 182, 213, 244, 274, 305, 335),
    labels = month.abb,
  ) +
  theme_minimal() +
  theme(
    panel.border = element_rect(fill = NA, linewidth = 0.5),
    axis.ticks = element_line(linewidth = 0.25),
    legend.title = element_blank(),
    legend.text = element_text(size = 14),
    legend.position = "top",
    strip.text = element_text(size = 14),
    axis.title = element_text(size = 12),
    axis.text = element_text(size = 10),
    panel.grid.minor = element_blank(),
    panel.grid.major.x = element_blank(),
    panel.grid.major.y = element_line(linewidth = 0.25, color = "gray80")
  )









Saturation is achieved across sites and years, indicating that the majority of adult lizards have likely been captured at least once during the field seasons.

Inter-annual survival probabilities
To evaluate population persistence across seasons, we compute empirical apparent survival probabilities for adult males by tracking whether an individual detected in a given year was recaptured in the subsequent year. The resulting annual survival rates are plotted across consecutive yearly transitions for high- and low-elevation populations.


Plot inter-annual survival probabilities
mr_data_adults |&gt;
  select(site, id, sex, year) |&gt;
  distinct() |&gt;
  group_by(site, sex, id) |&gt;
  mutate(all_years = list(year)) |&gt;
  rowwise() |&gt;
  mutate(
    surv_next_year = as.numeric((year + 1) %in% all_years)
  ) |&gt;
  ungroup() |&gt;
  select(-all_years) |&gt;
  group_by(site, sex, year) |&gt;
  summarise(
    surv_next_year = mean(surv_next_year), .groups = "drop"
  ) |&gt;
  filter(year &lt; 2025) |&gt;
  filter(year &gt; 2022) |&gt;
  filter(sex == "M") |&gt;
  mutate(year = as.integer(year)) |&gt;
  mutate(surv_next_year = as.numeric(surv_next_year)) |&gt;
  ggplot(
    aes(
      x = paste(year, "to", year + 1),
      y = surv_next_year,
      color = site
    )
  ) +
  geom_line(aes(group = site), linewidth = 1) +
  geom_point(size = 3) +
  labs(
    x = "Interval",
    y = "Mean inter-annual survival"
  ) +
  scale_color_manual(values = site_colors) +
  theme_minimal() +
  theme(
    panel.border = element_rect(fill = NA, linewidth = 0.5),
    axis.ticks = element_line(linewidth = 0.25),
    legend.title = element_blank(),
    legend.text = element_text(size = 14),
    legend.position = "top",
    strip.text = element_text(size = 14),
    axis.title = element_text(size = 12),
    axis.text = element_text(size = 10),
    panel.grid.minor = element_blank(),
    panel.grid.major.x = element_blank(),
    panel.grid.major.y = element_line(
      linewidth = 0.25,
      color = "gray80"
    )
  )









